  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Decreasing storm coverage today before ramping\nup late in the week with a returning warm front.\n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms over central Pennsylvania\nwill track southeast and largely miss our area tonight, except\nperhaps Cecil County. Other showers tried to form earlier over\nShenandoah County, but have vanished. There is a low-level\nmoisture gradient seen on polar imagery roughly along Rt 15 and\nthe 00Z IAD sounding showed 759 J/kg of CAPE with little\ninhibition. There is a weak trough that is fcst to cross the\narea between now and 06Z, so there is still a chance some\nshowers or storms may develop, but the window is closing down.\nLightningCast v2 doesn't show any development imminent at this\ntime. The window for any development this evening is very short.\n\n\nHeights further build on Thursday into Friday as upper ridging \nstretches up from the Gulf of America into the central and \nsoutheastern U.S. Locally, being north of this ridge will favor \ncontinued northwesterly flow aloft which carries the risk of \nmesoscale convective systems (MCS) intrusions. On Thursday, the \nmain shower and thunderstorm chances focus across the \nAlleghenies down into the central Virginia Piedmont and I-64 \ncorridor. This largely ties to the front still being displaced \noff toward the south. Eventual northward progression is possible\ninto Friday which introduces a better chance for storms. The \nlatest ensembles support probabilities in the 40 to 70 percent \nrange. While not a washout by any means, this should be heavily \nguided by the peak in daytime heating. High temperatures each \nday will be in the 80s to low 90s, with 70s for the mountains. \nLikewise, seasonable conditions can be expected overnight.\n\nAmple high pressure builds to the north of the region on \nSaturday as the frontal zone again sags back toward the \nCarolinas. An onshore component to the wind could keep some\nadditional cloud cover around. However, overall trends suggest a\ndrop off in humidity before winds return to southerly late in\nthe weekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Showers and thunderstorms possible between\nSunday and Monday before becoming drier into early next week.\n\nUpper-level ridging continues to build over the Southwest CONUS over \nthe weekend, with multiple shortwave troughs tracking to the east \noffshore of New England. Beginning Sunday, an upper-level \nperturbation forms over the Canadian Rockies and tracks eastward, \nwith a stemming frontal boundary tracking across the East Coast late \nSunday and into Monday. This boundary will likely bring chances for \nscattered showers and thunderstorms here in the Mid-Atlantic, \nalthough model guidance has been somewhat marginal on the severe \npotential for this event. As of the 12z ensemble runs, several \noutputs have the highest likelihood for severe weather just south of \nthe area towards southern VA and the Carolinas, while the GFS \nensemble runs still have a 15%-30% probability for severe weather \ncentered within the region. Should any thunderstorms become severe, \nthe most likely hazards will likely be elevated wind gusts as well \nas locally heavy rainfall. As drought conditions continue to improve \nwithin the region, flooding potential becomes more of a concern with \nany heavy rainfall-producing thunderstorms that materialize. \n\nOtherwise, temperatures remain near-normal for mid-August, with \nhumid conditions as well as temperatures ranging in the 80s and \nlower-90s at times near the metros. High pressure moves into the \nregion once the frontal boundary passes offshore by Tuesday, \nbringing drier conditions to the region.",
